course: dictionary
lesson: 15888
title: Uninteresting and vivid words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Uninteresting means not interesting. It is dull and boring, so it does not hold your attention. The prefix un- means "not", the same as in unhappy or unkind.
explain: «The film was uninteresting, and I looked at my watch twice.» The watch-checking tells you the film was dull. When a text makes you want to stop, look at the words that made you feel that way.
explain: Writers avoid uninteresting words like «nice» and «good». «It was a nice day» tells the reader very little. «The sun blazed over the glittering sea» paints a picture, so the reader wants to keep reading.
fact: Uninteresting means not interesting; dull and boring.
fact: The prefix un- means "not", so uninteresting is the opposite of interesting.
fact: «The lesson was uninteresting, so Sam daydreamed all morning.»
fact: Plain words like «nice» and «good» are often uninteresting, because they do not show the reader anything specific.
fact: Vivid words, like «glittering» or «blazed», help a reader see, hear and feel the story.
exercise: «The film was uninteresting, and I looked at my watch twice.» What does uninteresting mean? | Exciting and full of surprises | Very loud and funny | *Dull and boring | Too long to finish
exercise: The prefix un- means "not". Which word is the opposite of uninteresting? | Plain | Boring | *Fascinating | Dull
exercise: Which sentence is more vivid, so it is less uninteresting? | *«The sun blazed over the glittering sea.» | «The day was fine.» | «It was okay, I guess.» | «It was a nice day.»
test: «The programme was so uninteresting that Mia fell asleep.» What does uninteresting mean here? | Noisy | Very short | Exciting and new | *Dull, so it did not hold her attention
test: Which word is the best replacement for «nice» in a story? | Good | *Sparkling | Fine | Okay
exam: Why should a writer avoid uninteresting words like «nice»? | To make the reader stop reading | To make the story longer | *To keep the reader's attention with vivid words | Because nice is a rude word
